WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:01.793
Wide ranging survey on how

00:00:01.793 --> 00:00:03.336
racism and discrimination

00:00:03.336 --> 00:00:04.462
affect American life

00:00:04.462 --> 00:00:05.630
finds that minorities

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:06.381
in this country

00:00:06.381 --> 00:00:09.050
expect to face discrimination

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:10.427
when they seek health care.

00:00:10.427 --> 00:00:12.053
They not only expected,

00:00:12.053 --> 00:00:13.722
they prepare for it.

00:00:13.722 --> 00:00:15.390
KFF, a nonprofit group

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:16.516
focused on health care

00:00:16.516 --> 00:00:18.601
policy, says that many Hispanic,

00:00:18.601 --> 00:00:20.020
black, Asian and American

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.480
Indian and Alaska Native adults in the US

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.107
believe they must modify

00:00:24.107 --> 00:00:27.068
both their mindset and the way they look

00:00:27.068 --> 00:00:29.154
to stave off potential mistreatment

00:00:29.154 --> 00:00:30.864
during health care visits.

00:00:30.864 --> 00:00:32.741
60% of black people surveyed,

00:00:32.741 --> 00:00:34.784
52% of American Indian

00:00:34.784 --> 00:00:35.702
and Native Alaskans,

00:00:35.702 --> 00:00:38.747
51% of Hispanics, 42% of Asian

00:00:38.747 --> 00:00:41.958
said that they prepare to hear insults

00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:44.127
or they feel that they have to take care

00:00:44.127 --> 00:00:45.879
extra care with their appearance

00:00:45.879 --> 00:00:47.505
in order to be treated fairly.

00:00:47.505 --> 00:00:49.424
At least some of the time.

00:00:49.424 --> 00:00:50.675
Joining me now to talk about

00:00:50.675 --> 00:00:52.635
this is Samantha Arteaga,

00:00:52.635 --> 00:00:55.221
KFC's director of racial equity

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:56.473
and health policy.

00:00:56.473 --> 00:00:58.141
Samantha, thank you for being with me.

00:00:58.141 --> 00:01:01.019
This implicit bias does not

00:01:01.019 --> 00:01:03.063
come as a surprise to some people,

00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:06.524
but to those who are new to this.

00:01:06.608 --> 00:01:08.026
Explain what you've learned

00:01:08.026 --> 00:01:09.319
about the different ways

00:01:09.319 --> 00:01:10.695
that whites and minorities

00:01:10.695 --> 00:01:11.446
in this country

00:01:11.446 --> 00:01:14.282
say that they experience health care

00:01:14.282 --> 00:01:16.576
differently.

00:01:16.659 --> 00:01:17.660
So overall,

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:19.079
I think the survey findings

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:20.121
really highlight

00:01:20.121 --> 00:01:21.664
how persistent and prevalent

00:01:21.664 --> 00:01:23.124
racism and discrimination

00:01:23.124 --> 00:01:24.000
remain in the U.S.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:24.793
today

00:01:24.793 --> 00:01:26.461
and how those experiences

00:01:26.461 --> 00:01:28.129
extend into health care,

00:01:28.129 --> 00:01:30.048
affecting people's experiences,

00:01:30.048 --> 00:01:30.965
seeking health care,

00:01:30.965 --> 00:01:32.842
as well as ultimately their health

00:01:32.842 --> 00:01:34.052
and well-being.

00:01:34.052 --> 00:01:35.178
As you mentioned,

00:01:35.178 --> 00:01:36.638
our survey findings highlight

00:01:36.638 --> 00:01:37.847
how experiences

00:01:37.847 --> 00:01:38.848
with racism

00:01:38.848 --> 00:01:40.934
can affect people's experiences

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:42.060
even before they get

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.353
to the doctor's office

00:01:43.353 --> 00:01:44.854
with them taking steps

00:01:44.854 --> 00:01:46.689
to prepare for these visits.

00:01:46.689 --> 00:01:48.316
Concerned about facing insults

00:01:48.316 --> 00:01:51.486
from a provider or office staff and also

00:01:51.486 --> 00:01:52.654
being extra careful

00:01:52.654 --> 00:01:53.655
about their appearance

00:01:53.655 --> 00:01:55.532
in order to be treated fairly.

00:01:55.532 --> 00:01:57.283
But even when folks get into the health

00:01:57.283 --> 00:02:00.078
care doctor's office, we see

00:02:00.078 --> 00:02:03.081
them take being treated unfairly

00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:03.998
more frequently

00:02:03.998 --> 00:02:05.625
due to their race and ethnicity,

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:07.585
and also saying that they have

00:02:07.585 --> 00:02:08.294
more certain

00:02:08.294 --> 00:02:09.671
negative experiences

00:02:09.671 --> 00:02:10.713
in the doctor's office

00:02:10.713 --> 00:02:12.465
because of their race and ethnicity.

00:02:12.465 --> 00:02:14.300
So things like a health care provider

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.678
not taking their concerns seriously,

00:02:16.678 --> 00:02:18.346
not answering a question

00:02:18.346 --> 00:02:20.890
or responding to a request,

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:23.518
blaming them for a health issue or not

00:02:23.518 --> 00:02:24.936
prescribing pain medication

00:02:24.936 --> 00:02:26.563
that they thought they needed.

00:02:26.563 --> 00:02:27.397
You know, it's interesting

00:02:27.397 --> 00:02:29.315
I found in this survey

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:32.068
is that the minority respondents

00:02:32.068 --> 00:02:35.113
didn't think that it would be any better

00:02:35.363 --> 00:02:37.115
if the health care providers

00:02:37.115 --> 00:02:39.367
shared their race or ethnicity.

00:02:39.367 --> 00:02:41.536
So this is not a

00:02:41.536 --> 00:02:44.497
a white provider black patient.

00:02:44.664 --> 00:02:47.667
It is the medical services in

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:48.585
the industry.

00:02:48.585 --> 00:02:51.254
It is the medical profession

00:02:51.254 --> 00:02:52.547
and the patient

00:02:52.547 --> 00:02:54.132
as it relates to minorities.

00:02:54.132 --> 00:02:56.134
Explain that a bit more.

00:02:56.217 --> 00:02:57.594
Well, it's interesting, even though

00:02:57.594 --> 00:02:59.345
survey respondents said that

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:01.055
they didn't think they would get better

00:03:01.055 --> 00:03:03.141
care if they had a health care provider

00:03:03.141 --> 00:03:04.309
who looked like them.

00:03:04.309 --> 00:03:05.351
What we actually see

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:06.686
in the survey findings

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:09.105
is that when adults of color

00:03:09.105 --> 00:03:10.315
have more visits

00:03:10.315 --> 00:03:11.608
with a health care provider

00:03:11.608 --> 00:03:12.817
who shares their racial

00:03:12.817 --> 00:03:14.152
and ethnic background,

00:03:14.152 --> 00:03:15.361
they report having

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:16.905
more frequent positive,

00:03:16.905 --> 00:03:18.198
respectful interactions

00:03:18.198 --> 00:03:19.908
with their health care provider.

00:03:19.908 --> 00:03:21.159
So the survey findings

00:03:21.159 --> 00:03:21.910
actually do

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:24.120
point to some potential opportunities

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:27.916
and benefit from increasing efforts to

00:03:27.999 --> 00:03:29.250
improve the diversity

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:31.002
of the health care workforce.

00:03:31.002 --> 00:03:33.087
But we also see that the survey findings

00:03:33.087 --> 00:03:34.088
are pointing widely

00:03:34.088 --> 00:03:35.590
to the importance of continued

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:37.425
training and education programs

00:03:37.425 --> 00:03:39.510
to root out the bias and discrimination

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:41.054
that are reflected in the survey.

00:03:41.054 --> 00:03:43.056
Yeah, and I think we take that

00:03:43.056 --> 00:03:47.268
finding and mirror that against

00:03:47.352 --> 00:03:48.937
what we see in some states,

00:03:48.937 --> 00:03:50.230
specifically in Florida

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:51.231
with Governor Ron

00:03:51.231 --> 00:03:54.234
DeSantis, who defunded DEA,

00:03:54.275 --> 00:03:55.193
the Diversity,

00:03:55.193 --> 00:03:57.612
Equity and Inclusion programs

00:03:57.612 --> 00:04:01.282
at schools, at learning institutions,

00:04:01.366 --> 00:04:02.909
de-funding the implicit

00:04:02.909 --> 00:04:05.036
bias training at these places

00:04:05.036 --> 00:04:09.457
so that any plan, any effort to lessen

00:04:09.457 --> 00:04:09.958
at least what

00:04:09.958 --> 00:04:11.626
we're seeing through this survey

00:04:11.626 --> 00:04:13.336
in some states is really

00:04:13.336 --> 00:04:16.589
facing some major challenges.

00:04:16.673 --> 00:04:18.758
Overall, I think the survey findings

00:04:18.758 --> 00:04:19.842
really point

00:04:19.842 --> 00:04:20.885
to the importance

00:04:20.885 --> 00:04:22.971
of continued action to address

00:04:22.971 --> 00:04:24.764
racism and discrimination,

00:04:24.764 --> 00:04:26.975
particularly in health care.

00:04:26.975 --> 00:04:29.227
They show that racism and discrimination

00:04:29.227 --> 00:04:30.937
are not just a social issue,

00:04:30.937 --> 00:04:32.689
they are a health issue.

00:04:32.689 --> 00:04:33.982
And beyond the findings

00:04:33.982 --> 00:04:35.775
reflected in terms of impacts

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:36.985
on people's experiences

00:04:36.985 --> 00:04:38.194
seeking health care,

00:04:38.194 --> 00:04:39.487
they also highlight

00:04:39.487 --> 00:04:42.448
the strong link between discrimination

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:44.534
and health and well-being.

00:04:44.534 --> 00:04:46.661
So people who report more experiences

00:04:46.661 --> 00:04:48.037
with discrimination

00:04:48.037 --> 00:04:49.414
in their daily life,

00:04:49.414 --> 00:04:51.249
which remains frequent,

00:04:51.249 --> 00:04:52.750
are more likely to report

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:53.459
certain negative

00:04:53.459 --> 00:04:54.294
health impacts

00:04:54.294 --> 00:04:55.503
like increased feelings

00:04:55.503 --> 00:04:59.257
of anxiety, depression and loneliness.

00:04:59.257 --> 00:05:01.301
So there are health impacts

00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:03.094
also tied to these experiences

00:05:03.094 --> 00:05:04.429
with discrimination.

00:05:04.429 --> 00:05:05.930
About more than I experience

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:06.764
saying discomfort.

00:05:06.764 --> 00:05:07.932
It's about outcomes

00:05:07.932 --> 00:05:09.726
for these communities as well.

00:05:09.726 --> 00:05:10.935
Samantha Arteaga with

00:05:10.935 --> 00:05:12.395
KFF, thank you so much.

